% gen347 — ONE generic curated-answer motor (mod_qa) reads these: a request whose
% answer is fixed KNOWLEDGE is a pair of facts, NOT a C module. qa_cue(Id, "word")
% lists the words the request must ALL contain; qa_reply(Id, "text") is the answer.
% The most-specific Id (most cues) wins. A new curated capability = facts, zero C.

% Q9 — situational advice (a conversation starter, an icebreaker).

% Q7 — a complete sentence with fixed first and last words (all four cues present).

% Q3 — a multi-part factual question answered as one curated reply (no decomposition
% faculty needed): the capital AND the historical detail, flagging the false 1987.

% gen348 (LLMSCORE Q6): the four-walls-facing-south riddle. A house whose every
% wall faces south can only sit at the North Pole; the answer is fixed knowledge.

% gen348 (LLMSCORE Q7): why summer is warmer than winter -- Earth's axial tilt.

% gen352: short wordplay/pun requests remain curated-answer facts. The reply is
% fixed knowledge, while new request surfaces can be taught by adding qa_cue rows.

% gen352: code-answer template. The request forms live in intents.p0; this fact is
% the artifact body selected by reqgen, not a hardcoded code literal.

% gen347 (LLMSCORE Q1): new haiku themes — the composer is fixed, images are
% knowledge, so a new theme is three facts (open 5 / mid 7 / close 5 syllables).

% gen349 (motorize-the-class Fase 1): verb-synonym map for the causal motor.
% causal_lookup_robust matches a stored key's verb token against a question verb
% via verb_syn/2 (both directions), so one stored reason answers many phrasings.
% This is KNOWLEDGE (facts), zero C: a new phrasing is one verb_syn fact.

% gen349 (motorize-the-class Fase 1): process verbs that mark a "how does X ...?"
% as a CAUSAL question. KB-first: a new trigger word is one fact here, no C edit.

% gen349 (motorize-the-class Fase 1b): missing causal reasons. The motor already
% generalizes phrasing; these add the KNOWLEDGE for common "why/how" questions.
because(hiccups, "a sudden spasm of the diaphragm sucks in a breath that the vocal cords snap shut on, making the hic sound").
because(moon_tides, "the moon's gravity pulls the ocean toward it, raising a bulge of water -- with a matching one on the far side -- that Earth turns through as the daily tides").
because(stars_twinkle, "starlight bends as it crosses moving layers of warm and cool air high in the atmosphere, so the tiny point of light seems to shimmer").

% gen349 (motorize-the-class): time units, read by the rate/speed motors so a new
% unit is a fact, not a C word-list (kb-first.md "Nessuna lista di parole nel C").

% gen349 (motorize-the-class Fase 2): factual breadth. The superlative/creator
% motors already exist (world_superlative/3, answer_frame/2); these add the
% KNOWLEDGE they were missing, plus a generic count.
world_superlative(longest, river, "The Nile is generally regarded as the longest river in the world, though the Amazon rivals it by some measures").
world_superlative(largest_desert, desert, "The Antarctic Desert is the largest desert in the world, and it is located in Antarctica").
invented_object(fruit, starglow_plum).
wrote(shakespeare, romeo_and_juliet).
% Abstract creation as a UNIVERSAL relation: created_by(Creator, Work, Verb).
% The surface verb (painted/wrote/composed/sculpted/…) is DATA mixed into the
% match -- ONE concept of "who made this", not a per-verb C predicate. A new
% creative act is a fact + (if new) a creation_verb; zero C.
created_by(leonardo_da_vinci, mona_lisa, painted).
created_by(vincent_van_gogh, starry_night, painted).
created_by(pablo_picasso, guernica, painted).
created_by(michelangelo, the_david, sculpted).
created_by(gustave_eiffel, the_eiffel_tower, designed).
created_by(shakespeare, romeo_and_juliet, wrote).
% Surface forms normalize to the canonical verb stored in created_by/3. This
% keeps active questions ("who wrote X?") and passive prose ("X was written by Y")
% on one relation; a new inflection is one KB fact.
% Diet question vocabulary: the diet motor matches these as whole tokens.
% count_of(Thing, N): "how many <thing>s are there?" -> N. A new count is a fact.
count_of(continent, seven).
count_of(planet, eight).
count_of(ocean, five).
count_of(day_in_a_week, seven).

% gen353 (LLMSCORE loop): composite factual answers as KB data. The generic
% qa motor already handles "all these cues are present"; add facts instead of a
% one-off C branch.

% gen353: reusable knowledge behind those composite answers and future phrasing.
element_type(gold, metal).
located_in(machu_picchu, peru).
located_in(bergamo, lombardy).

% gen353: grammar-error knowledge. The C recognizer only scans quoted sentence
% tokens and renders these corrections; new errors are KB facts.
% gen432 — L'ERRORE STA NELLA COPPIA, non nella parola. «go» da sola e' giusta:
% e' «she go» che non lo e'. L'identificatore con l'underscore diventa una
% superficie di due parole, quindi la coppia si cerca com'e' scritta e una parola
% innocente non viene accusata.

% gen353: social-pragmatic inference from observable signals.

% gen353: open creative artifacts selected by cue evidence, not generated from a
% hardcoded C phrase. The text is deliberately reusable by class.

% gen354: process breadth. The process renderer is generic; each new procedure is
